# Filament - strings, and the first pass over them with the harness pointed
# at the machine rather than at the bank.
#
# **Six things in the model were wrong and all six are fixed.** They are the
# same three lessons the brass and the woodwinds gave, in a third dress.
#
#   - **The loop length was guessed, not solved.** It was the period minus a
#     *DC estimate* of two of the four things in the loop. But it is the
#     phase at the note that decides where a string sounds, and an allpass
#     has its full delay at DC and less at every frequency above; the damper
#     tap was not counted at all. A damper a third of the way along put the
#     note fifty cents up. Solved from the whole loop's phase now, the tap
#     included, which takes three passes because the tap sits along the
#     length being solved for.
#   - **An allpass at zero stiffness is still a one-sample delay.** Two of
#     them on a 367-sample string is nine cents, and every patch had it.
#   - **Coupling was a gain.** Two strings over one bridge were each handed
#     the other's whole output every sample: over a 145-sample turn at
#     `couple` 0.8 that added two and a third to a loop gain already at
#     0.999. The Wire patch was fourteen decibels louder with coupling on
#     than off and its note-on read *fifty-four thousand times* the edge of
#     its own tone. A bridge shares rather than makes, so each string now
#     gets a fraction of the *difference*, and the fraction is of a turn.
#   - **The sympathetic bank had the same fault**, six strings each given
#     eight per cent of the played note every sample. They never stopped.
#   - **Stiffness could bend the loop by a third of the string.** A real wire
#     is sharp in its partials by a fraction of a percent low down and a few
#     percent at the top. A third is not a stiff string, it is a different
#     instrument every note. A twelfth at full stiffness now.
#   - **A string cannot hold DC, and this one could.** Nothing blocked it and
#     the loop passes DC whole, so a hammer - whose blow is a squared sine
#     and therefore never negative - and a jet, whose pressure has an offset
#     by definition, each left a standing offset going round for ever. A
#     seventh of full scale on the sympathetic patch. It was headroom spent
#     on nothing, a thump at every note, and enough of a pedestal that the
#     harness could not find a pitch in six of these ten patches at all.
#
# **And how long a string rings is a time, not a loop gain.** A fixed loss per
# turn is a string that goes round f times a second, so the same number gave
# a low string twice the ring of the one an octave up. Two octaves above where
# a patch was voiced there was nothing left to measure. `sustain` is now
# seconds - a twentieth at nothing, eight at the top - and `sustainkey` does
# what it says instead of the opposite.
#
# Measured, against the bank this replaces: DC from -17 dB to -110, note-ons
# from 54076x and 1346x and 35x down to 17.8x at the worst, and eight of ten
# patches have a findable pitch where four did. Every one of those eight is
# within 16 cents. The plucked patches are levelled on peak and the sustained
# ones on rms, because a bank holding both cannot be levelled on one number -
# the "loudness spread" line is reading the two against each other and should
# be ignored here.
#
# **The second pass took the three that were left.**
#
#   - **The bow played two and a half octaves under the note.** Not
#     period-doubling: a waveguide's loop gain well below its own note is the
#     loop filter's, near one, and a bow adds negative resistance on top of
#     that. The total went over one and turned the corner of the loop's own
#     DC blocker into a relaxation oscillator - an eleven-hertz wander with
#     the note underneath it. The blocker sits at a tenth of whatever note is
#     playing now (`kDcBelow`), which a string can say for itself: it has no
#     motion below its own fundamental. The bow reads +4 cents.
#   - **A pick was noise times an alternating plus and minus one** - a square
#     wave at Nyquist, which does not brighten a spectrum, it inverts it, and
#     puts the whole burst where no string has a partial. Wire started twelve
#     times brighter than it continued and nothing on the string could reach
#     it, because none of it was on the string. A pick is a harder, narrower
#     contact, so it is a first-order tilt now.
#   - **`sustain` did not mean seconds.** Asked for two it rang for three
#     quarters of one, because the tone filter and the DC blocker take their
#     own share every turn and nothing counted it. The string works out what
#     to ask its feedback for so that a whole turn comes to what the caller
#     said - the same reason the brass and the woodwinds solve their loops.
#     Measured on the fundamental alone: Nylon 1.72 s against 2 asked, Wire
#     4.2 against 5. A stiff or tension-modulated string comes in shorter
#     still, which is what dispersion and a bending pitch do.
#
# And one the bank test caught on the way past: **a damper was a comb with
# peaks.** Subtracting a delayed tap returns `1 + k` where the tap comes back
# inverted, so a damper of 0.4 handed the loop a gain of 1.36 and the
# prepared patch grew a partial until it ended louder than it started. A
# finger can only take away; divided by that peak it still nulls the note at
# a node, which is the whole point of putting one there.
#
# **The last ten notes in the bank's ranges were two different things.** Nine
# were the damper on Prepared nulling the fundamental it sat under, so that
# the loudest thing in the note was the body ringing at its own pitch rather
# than the string at the played one - `harm` read 0.00 to 0.10 and taking
# `bodymix` to zero brought the note straight back. The damper is halfway
# along now, where it nulls the odd harmonics instead and every note returns.
# The tenth was Blown at note 84, in tune and on the right partial but nine
# parts jet noise to one part note, so its range stops at 82. All 379 notes
# of this bank now pass.
#
# **The spike in front of the note was a pulse train.** Nylon put one
# millisecond of excitation into a six millisecond loop, so what came out was
# two milliseconds of sound and four of near silence, over and over, at the
# right pitch - thirty decibels between the loud part and the quiet part of
# every single period. A pluck displaces the *whole* string, and a
# waveguide's string is one period long, so a pluck fills the loop. Nylon's
# note-on went from five times the corner of its own tone to one, its loudest
# partial from the octave to the fundamental, and the note carries seven
# decibels more of its own energy.
#
# **And a hammer's contact is a fraction of the string's period**, which is
# why a piano's treble hammers are harder and lighter than its bass ones.
# Fixed at six milliseconds it was two thirds of a period at the bottom of a
# range and four whole periods at the top, and a pulse four periods long has
# nothing at the note to give it: Prepared was twelve decibels quieter at the
# top of its range than the bottom with an identical peak. It scales from
# middle C now, and hits harder as the contact shortens, because a hammer of
# one mass at one speed carries the same blow whatever it lands on.
#
# **What is still wrong.** `Spoken To` is played by the audio input and so
# measures as silence here, which is correct and means it cannot be
# auditioned without one. Steel is three decibels under the rest of the bank
# at full volume, because its energy sits in high partials rather than at the
# note. And `chiff` reads 1.0 whenever a patch's tail is under a twentieth of
# its peak, which means "not measured" rather than "no chiff" - do not read
# the plucked patches' 1.0 as a result.
#
# **`position` is a pick position now.** A string plucked a fraction b along
# its length gets harmonic n in proportion to sin(n.pi.b): every harmonic
# with a node under the pick is missing, which is why playing by the bridge
# is thin and nasal and over the hole is round. That is one comb on the
# excitation, `1 - z^-bL` over a string L samples long. The comment had said
# so all along and the code underneath handed the *second string of the
# course* a fraction less excitation instead - a level difference between two
# strings, and measurably nothing: moving `position` from 0.08 to 0.3 changed
# no column at all. It now moves the steel patch's loudest partial from its
# eighth harmonic to its third, and at the middle of the string it reads -21
# on `hollow`, which is the even harmonics gone and is exactly what plucking
# there does. A bow and a jet do not get it: they hold a force against the
# string for the whole note rather than acting at a point and leaving.
# The four patches that set it had values chosen while it did nothing, so
# they are chosen again. The harness's `pitch` is unreliable for a string
# picked near its bridge - Steel's fundamental sits eleven decibels under its
# own fifth harmonic, and a direct look at the spectrum says the note is
# eight cents flat where the column says a hundred and ninety-six. Scoring a
# whole harmonic series instead of walking down from the loudest peak was
# tried and is worse; the column is wrong for Steel and Wire and the machine
# is not. And `edge` says a plucked string is twenty times brighter at its
# attack than in its tail, which is true and is not a click.
#
# **Two more faults, found filling out the plucked, picked and struck
# families.** A hammer's blow was shaped over `length` while it lasted for
# the note's own contact time, so away from middle C it started part way
# into the bump or ran several bumps: a click on every struck note but one
# octave. It is one bump over the contact now, which took about seven
# decibels from Prepared's low notes, so its volume went up to match. And the feedback that makes
# up the DC blocker's loss at the note made up too much above it: with the
# tone open and a long ring the second and third partials went round at
# just over one and grew for ever, so a harp left ringing with its tone
# turned up ended twenty decibels louder than it started. The next few
# partials are kept under one; nothing that did not grow changed.
#
# The body is a light colour at any setting - from no body to all of it
# is under two decibels in any third of an octave - so the body macros
# darken the tone a little as well. And the second and third partials sit
# ten to twenty cents flat of their harmonics while the fundamental is
# within three, so the `tune` column reads as much as twenty cents flat on
# patches old and new.
#
# Plucks and picks are levelled on peak, at -8. The struck patches meet
# Hammered half way between its peak and its loudness, since a hard short
# blow and a long ring cannot match on both.
#
# Families in tab order: plucked (Init first), picked, struck, bowed, blown,
# sympathetic, prepared. Every plucked, picked and struck patch but Init
# carries eight named macros, and so do Bowed and Blown, which have a
# gentle vibrato that fades in after the note starts.
# Macro destinations are numbered as in Filament.h's MacroDest, so #8 is
# sustain, #47 vibrato depth and #49 pitch.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Filament` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Filament
# A melody rather than one held note: one note is what one note sounds
# like, and a bank can pass that patch by patch and still be the same
# sound all the way down.
role    tune

patch Init family=plucked

